the manual page project

a basic requirement: i want to see the man page
for List::BisectSearch(3pm) at:

    https://man.rapidcow.org/man/3/List::BisectSearch (for HTML)
    https://man.rapidcow.org/man/3/List::BisectSearch.3   (for nroff)
    https://man.rapidcow.org/man/3/List::BisectSearch.3pm (ditto)


WHAT IS HERE

    catalog       what is published: releases (a tag of a Git
                  repository) and pages (NAME(SECTION) from a file
                  of a release)
    src/          the files of those pages, as ./snapshot took them
                  from the tags; src/DIST/VERSION/RELEASE says which
                  commit that was, and when
    patch/        local fixes to them (see below)
    skel/         the templates (Template Toolkit) of the pages
    assets/       the style sheet
    Makefile.in   the rules, MANIFEST the goal (siteconf)
    mkman         makes the pages: POD to HTML and nroff, links
                  between them, and the pages that list them
    checklinks    sees that every link in the site goes somewhere
    view          serves docs/ the way GitHub Pages does
    snapshot      copies what catalog says from the repository


BUILDING

    ./configure -Db=docs
    siteconf -gA -G gmake
    gmake docs           # make, if yours is GNU Make 4 (not macOS's 3.81)
    ./checklinks docs
    ./view               # http://localhost:8080/

You need siteconf with 561e580 (file names with colons; see
.github/workflows/pages.yml), GNU Make, and Template Toolkit; the rest (Pod::Simple, Pod::Man, JSON::PP) is core.
mkman fails the build when an L<> to one of our pages (or a section
of one) goes nowhere: that is how a broken cross-reference is caught.

render, which `siteconf -i -P web' wrote, is not used (yet): every
page is generated from data, by mkman.  ./upgrade still keeps it.


URIS

    /                                   home
    /man/                               every page
    /man/3/                             one section
    /man/3/RdKw::WwW::GenLib            a page (.html)
    /man/3/RdKw::WwW::GenLib.3pm        the same, for man(1) (and .3)
    /man/1/siteconf                     (and siteconf.1)
    /dist/WwW-GenLib/                   a distribution: its releases,
                                        and the pages of the newest

/man/ is always the newest release of each dist.  An older release,
once there is one, goes under its dist, the same way down:

    /dist/WwW-GenLib/0.02_03/                      (reserved)
    /dist/WwW-GenLib/0.02_03/man/3/RdKw::WwW::GenLib

(mkman already makes the stem of an older page that way.  Not done
yet: xref.json only has the newest pages, so links from an older
page go to the newest ones; and there are no rules for them, nor
pages that list them.)  Should there ever be other languages, they would be a
prefix on top of all this (/zh/man/3/...): English, which has none,
is the default.

A page is made under its own name, man/3/RdKw::WwW::GenLib.html,
which takes GNU Make (siteconf -G gmake; POSIX make cannot name a
file with a colon).  In Makefile.in and MANIFEST it is written
${"RdKw::WwW::GenLib"}, since a bare `:' would end a rule's targets;
in a `# HAVE:' header, RdKw${U+003A}${U+003A}WwW..., since there it
would end the name.  A link to it is written ./RdKw::WwW::GenLib, or
it would be read as a URL of scheme RdKw:.  Links have no .html at
the end (GitHub Pages finds them), unless site_url is not http(s).


ADDING A PAGE OR A RELEASE

 1. catalog: a `release' line (for a new tag), `page' lines.
 2. ./snapshot (or ./snapshot DIST), and commit src/.
 3. mkman: a NEED line for the new src/DIST/VERSION/RELEASE.
 4. Makefile.in: a rule for the cache (${c}/man/S/NAME.pod), and the
    page in the lists of xref.json, of the HTML pages, of the nroff
    pages; dist/DIST/index.html for a new dist.  MANIFEST: the same.

A newer release of a dist replaces the older one in /man/: change
VERSION in its page lines (and the paths in Makefile.in) to the new
one, keeping the older release line (for the list of releases).


PATCHING A PAGE

What is in src/ is as it was released; a fix goes in a patch, which
is applied to the copy in the cache (.cache/man/S/NAME.pod):

    patch/man/3/RdKw::WwW::SiteConf.pod.patch

Make it against the file in src/ (diff -u), and begin it with a
header that names it, and says why:

    # HAVE: patch/man/3/RdKw${U+003A}${U+003A}WwW${U+003A}${U+003A}SiteConf.pod.patch
    (why, in a few lines)
    --- a/src/...
    +++ b/src/...

(The header is not only for us: siteconf reads the first lines of a
prerequisite for one, and finds the POD's own `# HAVE:' examples in
a diff that has none.)  Then make it a prerequisite of that cache
rule in Makefile.in, and pass it to mkman prep, as is done for
RdKw::WwW::SiteConf.  mkman refuses to build a page whose patch is
not a prerequisite, and the page says that it is patched.
